  "text": "The user wants a plain-text paragraph of about 150 words about how rivers shape valleys. They explicitly said not to use any tools. I should reply directly in plain text as per the agent instructions.Rivers shape valleys by moving water and sediment downhill for thousands of years. Where the slope is steep, a river cuts a narrow channel and the valley walls stay close, often forming a V-shaped cross section. As the gradient flattens, the current slows, the channel widens, and the river begins to swing from side to side. Those bends undercut the outer bank and drop sand and gravel on the inner bank, so the floodplain grows while the valley floor broadens. Floods spread silt across that floor and rebuild the soil. Tributaries join the main stream and deepen side valleys, and the whole network lowers the land between the ridges. Hard rock resists longer than soft rock, so waterfalls, rapids, and uneven steps appear where the bed changes. Over time the river may abandon old meanders, leave terraces above the new channel, and carry the eroded material toward a lake or the sea. The valley you see is the record of that continuous cutting, widening, and depositing.",
